  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Credential Access / Forge Web Credentials (Golden Ticket)", "technique": "T1558.001", "what_happened": "At 2028-01-17 02:32:18 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 fired on WS-CONSOLE-01 (10.30.6.41): mimikatz.exe was launched with parentImage=cmd.exe under user CORP\\local-admin. The full commandLine: 'mimikatz.exe \"kerberos::golden /user:fakeadmin /domain:corp.local /sid:S-1-5-21-1234567890-1234567890-1234567890 /krbtgt:8e4d39f2c5b7a8e9f0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7 /id:500 /groups:512,513,518,519,520 /ptt\"'. This is a textbook Golden Ticket forge: the attacker possesses the krbtgt NTLM hash (obtained via prior DCSync or NTDS.dit extraction) and is now forging a Kerberos TGT entirely OFFLINE on a workstation. The TGT impersonates non-existent admin 'fakeadmin' with all top-tier group SIDs embedded (512=Domain Admins, 513=Domain Users, 518=Schema Admins, 519=Enterprise Admins, 520=Group Policy Creator Owners). The /ptt flag injects the forged TGT directly into the current logon session ticket cache. The krbtgt-hash compromise means EVERY DC in the forest is forever-untrusted until krbtgt password is rotated TWICE.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 with image=mimikatz.exe AND commandLine containing 'kerberos::golden' AND '/krbtgt:' AND '/ptt' — these tokens together are the Golden Ticket forge signature (MITRE T1558.001). Even with renamed mimikatz, the kerberos::golden command-string is the load-bearing indicator.", "first_seen": "2028-01-17T02:32:18+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Defense Evasion (Pre-forge Cache Cleanup)", "technique": "T1070", "what_happened": "At 02:34:42, Sysmon Event 1: klist.exe was launched with commandLine 'klist purge' under the same CORP\\local-admin session on WS-CONSOLE-01. mimikatz best practice before injecting a Golden Ticket is to PURGE the existing ticket cache so the forged TGT is the ONLY ticket the OS will use for subsequent Kerberos operations. The combination 'kerberos::golden /ptt' followed by 'klist purge' (or vice-versa) is a Golden Ticket workflow fingerprint.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=klist.exe + commandLine='klist purge' + user=CORP\\local-admin within 3 minutes of the mimikatz launch. The temporal proximity + same-session origin make this part of the same attack chain.", "first_seen": "2028-01-17T02:34:42+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Credential Access / Anomalous TGT Issuance", "technique": "T1558.001", "what_happened": "At 02:36:12, Windows Security Event 4768 fired on DC-CORP-01 (10.30.2.10): 'A Kerberos authentication ticket (TGT) was requested' — TargetUserName=fakeadmin, TicketEncryptionType=0x17 (RC4-HMAC, the LEGACY weak algorithm — modern AD uses AES 0x12/0x13), TicketOptions=0x40810010, **TicketLifetime=87600 hours = 10 YEARS** (Windows default Kerberos TGT lifetime is 10 HOURS). The 10-year lifetime is the GOLDEN-TICKET SMOKING GUN — no legitimate Kerberos workflow ever issues a TGT with 87,600-hour lifetime. NOTE: In a pure Golden Ticket scenario the TGT is forged OFFLINE on the workstation, so a real 4768 may not appear unless the attacker uses certain ticket-renewal paths. If observed, it indicates the forged TGT is being processed by the KDC.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4768 on DC-CORP-01 with TargetUserName=fakeadmin AND TicketLifetime=87600 (or any value >24h, well beyond the Windows default of 10h). The TicketLifetime field is the load-bearing detection — any TGT with lifetime >>10h is anomalous.", "first_seen": "2028-01-17T02:36:12+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Initial Access via Forged Identity / Non-Existent User Logon", "technique": "T1078", "what_happened": "At 02:36:14, Windows Security Event 4624 fired on DC-CORP-01: 'An account was successfully logged on' — TargetUserName=fakeadmin, AuthenticationPackageName=Kerberos, ImpersonationLevel=Impersonation, IpAddress=10.30.6.41 (WS-CONSOLE-01). **CRITICAL ANOMALY: fakeadmin does NOT exist as a real user in Active Directory** — the attacker invented this username during the Golden Ticket forge. A successful Kerberos logon for a username that doesn't exist in AD is impossible under normal Kerberos protocol (KDC would return KRB-AP-ERR-USER-UNKNOWN); the only way it can happen is via a forged TGT where the KDC trusts the embedded PAC without re-verifying user existence (this is the Kerberos design flaw Golden Ticket exploits).", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624 on DC-CORP-01 with TargetUserName=fakeadmin + AuthenticationPackageName=Kerberos + IpAddress=10.30.6.41, AND fakeadmin NOT present in the AD user list (Wazuh decoder cross-checks 4624 TargetUserName against a maintained AD user enumeration).", "first_seen": "2028-01-17T02:36:14+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Lateral Movement / Service Ticket Request", "technique": "T1550.003", "what_happened": "At 02:37:08, Windows Security Event 4769 fired on DC-CORP-01: 'A Kerberos service ticket was requested' — TargetUserName=fakeadmin, ServiceName=cifs/FILESVR-FIN-01.corp.local, TicketEncryptionType=0x17 (RC4). The forged TGT is now being USED to request a service ticket for SMB/CIFS access to FILESVR-FIN-01 (the finance fileserver). Each 4769 from fakeadmin is the attacker exercising the Golden Ticket — every service in the forest is a one-step-away target.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4769 on DC-CORP-01 with TargetUserName=fakeadmin (non-existent user) + TicketEncryptionType=0x17 (RC4 is deprecated and unusual in modern AD). The combination 'non-existent TargetUserName + RC4 encryption + service ticket request' is the Golden Ticket usage fingerprint.", "first_seen": "2028-01-17T02:37:08+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Lateral Movement / Privileged Logon on Target", "technique": "T1550.003 + T1078", "what_happened": "At 02:38:42, Windows Security Event 4624 fired on FILESVR-FIN-01 (10.30.12.55): 'An account was successfully logged on' — TargetUserName=fakeadmin, LogonType=3 (Network), AuthenticationPackageName=Kerberos, MemberOf includes the Domain Admins SID (S-1-5-21-...-512). The group SIDs were FORGED inside the Golden Ticket PAC — the attacker is now authenticated as a Domain Admin on the finance file server, despite fakeadmin not existing in AD and never having been granted Domain Admin membership through any legitimate process. The PAC-based group injection is the Golden Ticket's privilege escalation mechanism: the KDC trusts the PAC, and downstream services trust the KDC.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624 on FILESVR-FIN-01 with TargetUserName=fakeadmin + LogonType=3 + AuthenticationPackageName=Kerberos + Domain Admins SID in MemberOf. Lateral movement confirmed at the destination — the Golden Ticket has crossed from the forge host (WS-CONSOLE-01) through the KDC (DC-CORP-01) to an arbitrary target (FILESVR-FIN-01).", "first_seen": "2028-01-17T02:38:42+0100"}
  ],
